>Oh, BTW:  Ditto on rings of adornment.  Male characters should not be
>able to have their charisma increased by rings of adornment.  Men with
>gaudy jewelry aren't suddenly going to be treated better by avaricious
>shopkeepers.  The sparkle of the gem, moreover, might attract certain
>kinds of monsters, for better or worse.

First of all, rings of adornment are magical in nature, not merely physical,
so they will make any character more attractive.  (Y'know, magic, like in
_Excalibur_ when Merlin made Uther look like the Duke of Cornwall?)
Second, as has been explained in D&D guides and probably elsewhere, charisma
doesn't apply _only_ to one's sexual appearance and the viewer's reaction
(an elf might think an orc very handsome, considering it's a nasty, disgusting
orc, but would not want to have sex with it.)  Charisma is a matter of how well
groomed and mannered the person is, and how much the person appeals to the 
viewer's sense of taste.  An orc could be very charismatic among other orcs,
but still be viewed as uglier than sh*t by other races.  This doesn't even have
to go across racial barriers, but could be applied to social ones.  A knight
might think a wizard very weak and worthless, a northerner might look upon 
Southern charm with disgust, and so on.  Charisma is _more_ than just sex
(although it can have its influences---see succubi/incubi).  Third, not all
shopkeepers are male.  There are male and female alike, just as there are male
and female characters.  So there.  :-)
>Just a few ideas.
Just a few comments to your ideas--none intended as flames, merely a bit of
enlightenment. :->
